WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.495
In this episode, we discuss a very typical system issue: the code changes are minimal,

00:00:04.495 --> 00:00:09.272
but they are connected to model structure, inference scheduling, GPU memory paging, and external caching.

00:00:09.272 --> 00:00:14.774
The final error was a temporary draft state mistakenly treated as a valid prefix checkpoint,

00:00:14.774 --> 00:00:19.138
causing the model to continue generating from the erroneous state after the next request was restored.

00:00:19.138 --> 00:00:21.505
First, let's clarify the two types of caches.

00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:25.035
The attention cache stores keys and values for each token,

00:00:25.035 --> 00:00:28.762
while the recurrent layer maintains a continuously updated running state.

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:34.029
Both are in GPU memory and may enter the chunk allocator, but their semantics are different.

00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:37.294
Layers like Mamba and Gated Delta Net

00:00:37.294 --> 00:00:39.782
compress history into a fixed-size recurrent state.

00:00:39.782 --> 00:00:44.806
To recover from a specific token boundary, there must be a corresponding state for that boundary.

00:00:44.806 --> 00:00:48.395
The inference engine places the recurrent state into physical memory pages,

00:00:48.395 --> 00:00:52.184
while using logical slots to represent checkpoint positions in the sequence.

00:00:52.184 --> 00:00:56.768
Here, the zero block is not a valid all-zero state; it indicates that

00:00:56.768 --> 00:00:59.442
there is no recoverable state at this position.

00:00:59.442 --> 00:01:03.789
The recurrent state has a hard constraint: wherever a computation ends,

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:06.687
the system will naturally leave a state there.

00:01:06.687 --> 00:01:10.888
If a computation runs directly from 1616 to 4538,

00:01:10.888 --> 00:01:13.151
it indeed passed through 3232,

00:01:13.151 --> 00:01:15.898
but that does not mean a checkpoint was saved there.

00:01:15.898 --> 00:01:21.068
Multi-token prediction first drafts several candidate tokens, then lets the main model validate them in batches.

00:01:21.068 --> 00:01:25.332
The validated prefixes can be submitted, while the unaccepted candidates

00:01:25.332 --> 00:01:27.464
cannot prematurely pollute the official state.

00:01:27.464 --> 00:01:30.985
The key point here is that multi-token prediction does not just look at token IDs;

00:01:30.985 --> 00:01:36.092
it also requires the hidden states near the main model boundaries, as well as the draft module's own state.

00:01:36.092 --> 00:01:40.592
So when hitting a prefix, the inference engine deliberately skips the last block,

00:01:40.592 --> 00:01:43.592
allowing the current request to recompute that segment itself.

00:01:43.592 --> 00:01:47.674
The cache may indeed contain data for the first 3232 tokens,

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:52.998
but for multi-token prediction recovery, it only reports that it can directly skip to 1616.

00:01:52.998 --> 00:01:58.823
This step may seem conservative, but it ensures the correct hidden state and draft state.

00:01:58.823 --> 00:02:02.937
Bringing this constraint back to pre-filled scheduling changes the checkpoint stop.

00:02:02.937 --> 00:02:07.369
After enabling multi-token prediction, 1616 is already the starting point for the second step,

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:10.267
and it is no longer a future stopping point within the current computation.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:15.180
Thus, the second step will run directly from 1616 to 4538,

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.204
naturally skipping over the 3232 in between.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:22.112
Meanwhile, candidate paths also require the recurrent state.

00:02:22.112 --> 00:02:26.917
Until the results are out, these states can only reside in temporary draft memory pages.

00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:31.917
Temporary pages can reuse GPU memory, but they are not a shareable prefix checkpoint.

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:36.544
Then a relocation occurs: temporary page one leaves its logical position in the middle,

00:02:36.544 --> 00:02:40.801
the old position is zeroed out, and the same physical page is appended to the end for continued use.

00:02:40.801 --> 00:02:43.131
Note that there is no copying here.

00:02:43.131 --> 00:02:48.289
A non-zero physical number should not represent two valid positions within the same request simultaneously.

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:49.672
Here comes the problem.

00:02:49.672 --> 00:02:53.224
The connector of the external cache receives tail append information,

00:02:53.224 --> 00:02:56.402
it sees the newly added temporary page one and the next page,

00:02:56.402 --> 00:02:59.393
but it has not received the information that the old position has been zeroed out.

00:02:59.393 --> 00:03:02.985
Thus, the tracking table and the real block table begin to diverge.

00:03:02.985 --> 00:03:07.864
In the real table, the old position is zero, but the old tracking table still retains temporary page one.

00:03:07.864 --> 00:03:12.134
At this point, seeing a duplicate non-zero number, it cannot be treated as a shared state,

00:03:12.134 --> 00:03:15.692
but should be considered as evidence of relocation: the old position has become invalid.

00:03:15.692 --> 00:03:20.243
If the old tracking table continues, the external cache will save this temporary page

00:03:20.243 --> 00:03:24.066
as a valid state for position 3232.

00:03:24.066 --> 00:03:29.019
The next time the same prefix hits, this erroneous state will be loaded back into GPU memory,

00:03:29.019 --> 00:03:33.774
and the model will continue generating from the misaligned recurrent state, leading to stable corruption of the output.

00:03:33.774 --> 00:03:38.964
The fix is quite restrained: if a newly reported non-zero number has already appeared in the tracking table,

00:03:38.964 --> 00:03:42.184
the old position is first changed to empty, then appended to the new position.

00:03:42.184 --> 00:03:47.767
Zero can be repeated, so only repeated non-zero numbers are relocation signals.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:52.280
After zeroing, this recurrent state group will be recognized as an all-empty segment,

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.007
and the external cache will not fabricate a non-existent checkpoint.

00:03:56.007 --> 00:03:59.454
Missing a segment hit only requires a bit more computation.

00:03:59.454 --> 00:04:02.755
Restoring temporary states will compromise correctness.

00:04:02.755 --> 00:04:07.379
So this issue is not present in all mixed caches.

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:12.413
It requires recurrent layers, alignment patterns, multi-token prediction, temporary page relocation,

00:04:12.413 --> 00:04:14.721
and external caching to occur simultaneously.

00:04:14.836 --> 00:04:18.982
Finally, let's compress the entire chain: recurrent states need real boundaries,

00:04:18.982 --> 00:04:23.316
multi-token prediction forces the last block to recompute, the scheduler skips over intermediate boundaries,

00:04:23.316 --> 00:04:26.520
temporary pages move to the end, and the tracking table only sees appends.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.824
The fix maintains not a regular list, but a more serious semantic:

00:04:30.824 --> 00:04:35.816
whether this token boundary truly has a recoverable, shareable model state.

00:04:35.816 --> 00:04:40.816
Just remember this: correctly formatted memory does not necessarily represent a correct state.

00:04:40.816 --> 00:04:46.957
The caching system is not just moving data; it is also keeping track of where the model has actually run.
